      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Tested v32.0rc2 on Arch Linux x86_64 (kernel 7.2, GCC 16.2.1, Python 3.14), focusing on the privacy features (Tor, I2P, private broadcast, silent payments). No regressions found.\n\n**Verification and build**\n- `SHA256SUMS.asc`: 10 good builder signatures, 0 bad; source and `x86_64-linux-gnu` tarball hashes match.\n- Built from source with depends (`NO_QT=1`). The only compiler warnings were 18 instances of a known GCC `-Wstringop-overflow` false positive in Boost `multi_index` headers.\n\n**Test suites**\n- ctest: 377/377 passed (`script_assets_tests` skipped: no assets file), including `i2p_tests`, `torcontrol_tests`, `private_broadcast_tests`, `bip324_tests` and the libsecp256k1 `silentpayments` BIP352 vectors. The release `test_bitcoin` also passes.\n- Functional `--extended`: all 304 scripts pass on the source build. I reran the previously skipped ones (previous releases, IPC, bench, chainstate) and they pass. `feature_bind_port_*` and `rpc_bind.py --ipv4/--ipv6/--nonloopback` also pass in a network namespace with 1.1.1.5 / 1111:1111::5 assigned.\n- Ran the full functional suite again against the release binaries (`BITCOIND=...`): all pass. The single failure on that run (`rpc_getblockfrompeer.py`) was caused by a port collision with a local i2pd SAM listener; it passes 3/3 on rerun.\n- Privacy subset (31 scripts: `feature_proxy`, `feature_torcontrol`, `p2p_i2p_*`, `p2p_private_broadcast*`, `p2p_v2_*`, addr relay, `p2p_tx_privacy`, `rpc_net`, ...) run 10 times: 310/310 passed, no intermittent failures.\n- Not run: USDT tests (need root), `feature_utxo_abort_on_error` (plyvel), GUI, fuzz.\n\n**Live tests with release binaries (Tor 0.4.9.11, i2pd 2.61.0)**\n- Mainnet node with `-onlynet=onion -onlynet=i2p -listenonion -i2psam -i2pacceptincoming -privatebroadcast`: 10 outbound peers (5 onion, 5 I2P), all using v2 transport. `ADD_ONION successful (PoW defenses enabled)` (#33414). A persistent I2P destination was created, and the onion service could be reached from outside. I sampled the node's TCP/UDP sockets every second for the whole run and saw only loopback connections to Tor SOCKS, the Tor control port and I2P SAM. No clearnet connections and no DNS.\n- Private broadcast end to end over the real networks: I ran two isolated regtest nodes. The receiver was reachable only through its `.onion` / `.b32.i2p` address, and I synced its chain with `submitblock`. `sendrawtransaction` on the sender delivered the tx over I2P, and in a second run over Tor (~9 s). The tx was not in the sender's mempool until it was received back. `attempts_remaining` went from 1000 to 999 (#35680). With `-privatebroadcast` disabled, `getprivatebroadcastinfo` returns `-32601` (#35267).\n- I2P sessions still request `i2cp.leaseSetEncType=4,0`, consistent with the ElGamal sunset note (#35696).\n\n**Observations (all already known or tracked)**\n1. A transaction that is still in the private broadcast queue is silently dropped when the node restarts: it is in neither the mempool nor the queue afterwards. This is tracked in #34322, but neither the `sendrawtransaction` help text nor the release notes mention it. That may be worth a line in the notes, since the feature is new.\n2. #36312 (don't discourage private broadcast peers) and #36309 (clarify claims, mark as experimental) are milestoned for 32.0 but are not in rc2. They are pending in #36300, so I assume they will be in rc3.\n3. Release notes draft: the `-privatebroadcast` IP-leak fix entry cites #35319, which was closed unmerged. The fix was merged as #35410.\n4. Minor: when the only candidate address in addrman belongs to a peer the node already has a long-lived connection to, private broadcast refuses it (correct behavior) and retries about 10 times per second, logging a \"Failed to connect\" line each time. This only shows up in tiny test setups.\n",
